    <screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/filinq/main/img/screenshot-dashboard.png</screenshot>
    <screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/filinq/main/img/screenshot-consent.png</screenshot>
    <screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ConductionNL/filinq/main/img/screenshot-anonymization.png</screenshot>
    <!--
        Processing-activity export (AVG Art. 30) consumes OpenRegister's
        per-access read-logging capability (x-openregister-processing dialect,
        ProcessingLogService / ProcessingLogController). That capability ships
        from OpenRegister >= 0.2.14. The minimum is also recorded as the
        `openregister` constraint in lib/Settings/filinq_register.json.
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <php min-version="8.3" min-int-size="64"/>
        <database min-version="10">pgsql</database>
        <database>sqlite</database>
        <database min-version="8.0">mysql</database>
        <!--
        Every office format this app edits is a ZIP: `PackagePartIo` and
        `DocumentGuard` construct `ZipArchive` in five places, with no fallback
        and no capability check. Undeclared, that dependency surfaced only as a
        fatal at the moment someone opened a document, on an instance that had
        already installed the app successfully. Declared here, the install is
        refused up front, which is the whole point of the element.

        ⚠️ AFTER the <database> elements, not before. info.xsd fixes this order
        — php, database, command, lib, os, owncloud, nextcloud — and the first
        placement of this element sat between <php> and <database>, which fails
        validation outright. opencatalogi already declares the same lib in the
        same slot; copying its POSITION as well as its content would have
        skipped the error.
        -->
        <lib>zip</lib>
        <!--
        App dependency (not expressible in app-info.xsd): OpenRegister — Filinq's
        documents, templates and reports are OpenRegister objects, and
        src/manifest.json lists "openregister" as a required dependency.

        min-version is therefore 32, NOT 30: openregister declares min-version="32"
        because its lib/ContextChat/ContentProvider.php implements
        OCP\ContextChat\IContentProvider, an interface core does not have before
        Nextcloud 32. On NC 30-31 openregister refuses to install ("cannot be
        installed because it is not compatible with this version of the server"), so
        the app cannot function there. Declaring 30 advertised a range this app
        cannot deliver. Same defect as openconnector#1173.

        CI already tests stable32 only, so this changes the advertised app-store
        range and nothing about what runs.
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
    </dependencies>
    <background-jobs>
        <job>OCA\Filinq\BackgroundJob\SigningExpirationJob</job>
    </background-jobs>
    <!--
        info.xsd fixes the order of these elements: background-jobs, repair-steps,
        two-factor-providers, commands, settings, activity, dashboard,
        fulltextsearch, navigations. <commands> therefore belongs here, before
        <settings> — not after <navigations>, where it fails validation with
        "Element 'commands': This element is not expected."
    -->
    <!--
        docudesk -> filinq APP-ID RENAME (applies to BOTH blocks below).

        Nextcloud has no in-place app-id upgrade: changing <id> makes the server
        discover a DIFFERENT app. On a real deployment the old `docudesk` app is
        removed and `filinq` is installed fresh, so <install> — not just
        <post-migration> — is the hook this actually runs through. Both steps are
        registered under both hooks so neither path can miss them.

        `oc_appconfig` and `oc_preferences` are BOTH namespaced by app id, and
        they are different stores: MigrateAppConfigKeys covers the first,
        MigrateUserPreferences the second. Copying one does nothing for the
        other, which is why there are two steps and not one.

        ORDERING vs. settings initialisation. This app has no InitializeSettings
        repair step — SettingsInitializer runs from RegistrationBootstrap::boot()
        instead, i.e. only once the app is enabled and loaded for a request,
        which is strictly after every repair step of the install has run. The
        migrations therefore still land before the register import reads
        `configuration_version` out of the app-config namespace. If an
        InitializeSettings repair step is ever added, it MUST be listed AFTER
        these two: it would otherwise see an unconfigured app, re-import from
        scratch, and write fresh values that MigrateAppConfigKeys then refuses to
        overwrite — silently pointing the install at newly created, empty
        registers instead of the existing data-carrying ones.

        Both steps are idempotent and copy-only (no `docudesk` row is ever
        deleted), so they are safe no-ops on an instance that never had
        `docudesk` installed, and a rollback to the old app id still finds its
        data intact.
    -->
    <repair-steps>
        <post-migration>
            <step>OCA\Filinq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Filinq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
        </post-migration>
        <install>
            <step>OCA\Filinq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Filinq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
        </install>
    </repair-steps>
    <commands>
        <command>OCA\Filinq\Command\OfficeProbeCommand</command>
    </commands>
    <settings>
        <admin>OCA\Filinq\Settings\FilinqAdmin</admin>
        <admin-section>OCA\Filinq\Sections\FilinqAdmin</admin-section>
    </settings>
    <navigations>
        <navigation>
            <id>filinq</id>
            <name>Filinq</name>
            <route>filinq.dashboard.page</route>
            <icon>app.svg</icon>
        </navigation>
    </navigations>
